[Unit]
Description=Estate host checks — addresses, CPU caps, the host and pool inventory, latency, disk headroom, that the alert receiver delivers, that the mail server's outbound chain is wired, and that the mail domains' SPF/DKIM/DMARC resolve, and that every generated group compose still matches the member repos it is built from, and that only `authentik Admins` grants Authentik superuser rights, and that every sign-in gate's callback fits its proxy's header buffer, and that the DMARC aggregate reports show every sending stream aligning (the evidence behind tightening the policy to p=reject)
Documentation=https://github.com/innotelinc/innotel-platform-stack/blob/main/docs/container-placement.md

# Each check reads the estate hosts over ssh, so the LAN has to be up first. None of
# them asserts anything about a local service; they are the estate's own invariants.
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=/opt/innotel/estate-checks

# TEXTFILE_DIR is the directory node-exporter's textfile collector mounts (see
# extensions/monitoring/docker-compose.host.yml). Each check writes its own file there;
# the collector turns them into the metrics `prometheus/rules/estate-checks.yml` alerts
# on. The default is the Cerulean edge, which already runs the per-host metrics stack.
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/innotel/estate-checks.env
Environment=TEXTFILE_DIR=/root/cerulean-backup/authentik

# `-` on each ExecStart so a failing check does not stop the ones after it: all of them
# must publish every run, or a check that is merely unhappy today would take the others'
# metrics stale and page about the wrong thing. The finding is the textfile, not the
# unit's exit status. Reaching the hosts with a key is the clean case; where the estate
# uses a password instead, put SSHPASS in the environment file above (and install
# sshpass on this host). The key lives on the edge and a host is readable only when it is
# in that host's `authorized_keys` and named in the edge's `/root/.ssh/config` — see
# `scripts/trust-estate-hosts.py`, which installs both and whose `--check` reports drift.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-container-addresses.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-container-address.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-container-limits.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-container-limits.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-estate-inventory.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-estate-inventory.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-address-latency.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-address-latency.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-host-disk.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-host-disk.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-alert-delivery.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-alert-delivery.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-mail-auth.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-mail-auth.prom
# The mail server is reached at its own address (`mail`, 192.168.1.15, in the container
# address table), so the check keeps working when the container moves host — and it has:
# it is on `i3` since 2026-10-15, having been on `i1` then `i4` before that. The table is
# the authority for which host that is, not this comment. STALWART_USER/
# STALWART_PASSWORD come from the EnvironmentFile above; the check has no default
# credential and must never carry one.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-mail-relay.py --base http://192.168.1.15:8080 --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-mail-relay.prom

# The one check that is about this repo rather than about a host. The files in `groups/`
# are generated *and tracked*, so a member repo that moves on leaves the file a rebuild
# starts from behind — and this repo's CI only compares them when *this* repo changes,
# while the inputs live in the member repos. Both sides are taken from the remote: a
# shallow clone of the stack repo for the checked-in files, and `fetch-group-members.py`
# — the CI job's own step — for the member repos, so nothing here depends on a checkout
# on this host and a version of the scripts deployed years ago still compares that
# year's `main` against that year's repos. ~15 s a run (17 shallow clones, all small).
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-group-compose-freshness.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-group-compose-freshness.prom
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-authentik-privilege.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-authentik-privilege.prom

# A gate's callback, not its front page: oauth2-proxy's session cookie is larger
# than nginx's stock 4k header buffer, so a gated host answers 502 on the
# callback after the person has already signed in (2026-10-25). This probes
# every gated name's callback and reads each proxy block's buffer.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-sso-callbacks.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-sso-callbacks.prom

# The other half of the mail story: `check-mail-auth` proves the records are in the
# zone, this reads the reports receivers send back and says which sending streams do
# not align — the evidence behind tightening `_dmarc` to `p=reject`, which fails
# silently when it is wrong. DMARC_USER/DMARC_PASSWORD come from the EnvironmentFile
# above (the `rua` mailbox); the check has no default credential and must never carry
# one. Silence is read by age, not as a blanket "could not answer": within three days
# of the `rua` being published (--wired, default 2026-10-29) there is nothing to report
# on yet and it is a note, and after that it is a failure — the reporting path, not the
# policy, is what breaks that way. An unreadable mailbox is exit 2 either way.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-dmarc-alignment.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-dmarc-alignment.prom

# The edge's own two ways of losing a door without saying so (2026-10-29): a proxy
# host left pointing at a target that stopped existing (a loopback target from
# before the SSO gateway moved into its own network namespace — 502 on
# `admin.monarch.innotel.us` while the door beside it looked healthy), and a proxy
# host that drifted off the target `scripts/edge-vhost-targets.tsv` pins. Reads the
# generated blocks where the edge keeps them (the `proxy` container's volume) and
# TCP-connects every target; the discard port is reported as parked, not dead.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-edge-vhost-targets.py --config-dir /usr/src/proxy/data/nginx --expect /opt/innotel/estate-checks/edge-vhost-targets.tsv --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-edge-vhost-targets.prom

# And that the door still opens for the people it is for: drives
# `POST /api/tokens/sso` inside the edge container as the gateway's hop, where a
# learner in the required group has to get a token and an identity outside it — or
# one forged at the published admin port — has to be refused. The 2026-10-29
# sign-in outage was a peer map reading `$remote_addr` after realip had rewritten
# it; every case here still said 403, and nothing was watching.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-edge-sso-door.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-edge-sso-door.prom

# And that no NFS share has been mounted twice or gone stale. On 2026-10-29
# `/srv/onyx-pool` in `distro` held 32 769 entries: ganesha restarted, every handle
# went stale, `mountpoint` blocks on a stale handle instead of failing, and the
# minute timer therefore mounted another copy on top each run until every `df` on i3
# queued behind the probes and the control plane's health probe could not be
# answered. It reads each host and then each running container *inside* the
# container — a container's mountpoints are not paths on its host — and bounds every
# probe at five seconds.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/python3 /opt/innotel/estate-checks/check-mount-stacks.py --prom ${TEXTFILE_DIR}/estate-check-mount-stacks.prom
